# restic needs two things in the environment before any command works:
# RESTIC_REPOSITORY (where the backup lives) and a password. Without them every
# row below asks for a password on the terminal and half of them fail in a
# script. Put them in a file outside any repo and export them from there.
name:    restic
what:    encrypted, deduplicating backups. Snapshots, not copies
check:   restic
install: sudo dnf install restic   /   sudo apt install restic
docs:    restic help <command>, restic.readthedocs.io
probe:   help

= BEFORE ANYTHING WORKS
export RESTIC_REPOSITORY=... | the backend: a path, sftp:, s3:, rclone:
export RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE=... | a file holding the password, mode 600
restic init      | create the repository. Once, ever
restic check     | verify the repository is intact. Do this after a bad run

= MAKING A BACKUP
@restic backup   | snapshot a path — `restic backup ~/work`
  --exclude PAT  | skip anything matching
  --exclude-file F | ... with the patterns in a file
  --tag NAME     | label the snapshot so you can find it later
  --dry-run      | what it would store, storing nothing
  --one-file-system | do not cross into another mount

= LOOKING AT WHAT IS THERE
restic snapshots | every snapshot, newest last
restic snapshots --tag NAME | only the labelled ones
restic ls latest | the files in the newest snapshot
restic find FILE | which snapshots contain that file
restic diff ID1 ID2 | what changed between two snapshots
restic stats     | how much is actually stored after deduplication

= GETTING SOMETHING BACK
@restic restore  | write a snapshot back to disk
  latest --target DIR | the newest one, into a fresh directory
  ID --include PATH | only one path out of it
restic dump latest PATH | print one file to stdout, restoring nothing
restic mount DIR | browse every snapshot as a filesystem. The nicest way

= KEEPING IT FROM GROWING FOREVER
@restic forget   | drop snapshots by policy — this only unlinks them
  --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 12 | a sane policy
  --prune        | actually reclaim the space, in the same run
  --dry-run      | which snapshots the policy would remove
restic prune     | reclaim space from already-forgotten snapshots
restic unlock    | clear a stale lock left by an interrupted run

= ALL FLAGS (from --help)
